BANKS["Embedded C"] = BANKS["Embedded C"].concat([

// ---------------------------------------------------------- emb-errors
{ t: "emb-errors", d: 2, q: "A driver catches a timeout from the bus layer and returns a generic failure. What is lost?", o: ["Nothing: the caller only needs to know it failed", "The caller cannot tell a bus fault from no device", "Performance, since the caller has to retry blindly", "Type safety, because the code is now an int"], a: 1, why: "<p>The bus layer knew something specific: the transfer timed out. Flattening that to a generic failure discards information that only existed at that point.</p><p>The two cases need different responses and send an engineer to different places. A timeout points at wiring, pull-ups or a stuck slave. A NAK points at the wrong address or a part that is not fitted.</p><p>The rule is to propagate the callee's error unchanged unless you know something it did not. Adding your own code is justified when you have context the lower layer lacked, such as a read that succeeded but returned the wrong chip ID.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-errors", d: 2, q: "When is defining your own error code justified?", o: ["Whenever a function has more than one failure", "When you know something the callee did not", "Never: always pass through the underlying code", "Only for hardware faults, not logic errors"], a: 1, why: "<p>The bus layer reports that a read succeeded. Only the driver knows that the value it returned is not the identity this part should have. That is genuinely new information, so it earns a code.</p><p>The test to apply is whether a caller would branch on it. If the honest answer is that they would log it and carry on, it should be a log line, not a code.</p><p>A driver with fifteen error codes and three call sites that check any of them is worse than one with three codes that all get handled, because the unused codes create the appearance of error handling without the substance.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-errors", d: 1, q: "How do you decide the error set for a new API?", o: ["Enumerate everything that could possibly go wrong first", "Write the imaginary caller and count its branches", "Copy whatever the vendor SDK already defines", "One distinct code per function that can fail"], a: 1, why: "<p>Enumerating failures produces a long list from the implementation's point of view, most of which the caller cannot act on differently.</p><p>Writing the caller first inverts it: you discover that there are really three responses, retry, report the device as absent, and give up, so you need three codes.</p><p>This is a general design habit worth having beyond error codes. Designing an interface from the consumer's side rather than the implementation's side is most of what separates an API people can use from one they work around.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-errors", d: 2, q: "Why is goto acceptable for cleanup in C?", o: ["It is faster than a deeply nested if statement chain", "It gives one unwind path for every early exit", "MISRA requires it for resource release paths", "It avoids using stack for the cleanup"], a: 1, why: "<p>C has no destructors and no finally block, so a function that acquires several resources and can fail at each step has to release them somehow.</p><p>Duplicating the cleanup at every early return is where leaks come from: someone adds a fourth resource and updates three of the five exit paths.</p><p>Cascading labels at the end of the function give exactly one unwind sequence, and each early exit jumps to the label that releases precisely what it had acquired. The Linux kernel uses this pattern throughout, and it is a legitimate answer to the interview question about goto.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-errors", d: 2, q: "Which rule keeps goto-based cleanup safe?", o: ["Use it only inside an interrupt handler", "Forward only, to labels at the end", "At most one goto in any function", "Only with a matching label at the top"], a: 1, why: "<p>Forward-only jumps to labels at the end of the function produce a shape a reader can follow: the function proceeds downward, and every failure exits toward the same tail.</p><p>Backward jumps create loops that no loop construct describes, and jumps into the middle of a block skip declarations and initialisation. Those are what earned goto its reputation.</p><p>With the forward-only restriction the pattern is structured control flow with a different keyword, and reviewers can check it by eye.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-errors", d: 2, q: "Which is true of _Static_assert compared with assert()?", o: ["Both are evaluated at run time, on entry", "It costs nothing and cannot be disabled", "It can test the value of a variable", "It is slower, being a compiler pass"], a: 1, why: "<p>A static assert is evaluated by the compiler. It generates no instructions, occupies no flash, and a failing one produces a build error rather than a binary.</p><p>A runtime assert costs code and a string, is compiled out by NDEBUG in release builds so the check you relied on is absent exactly where it matters, and if left in can abort in front of a customer.</p><p>The limitation is that it can only test constant expressions: sizeof, offsetof, enum values, configuration macros. For anything involving a variable you need a runtime check, and then the question is what the system should do rather than whether to abort.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-errors", d: 2, q: "Where should argument validation live?", o: ["In every function, at every layer of the stack", "At API boundaries, trusting internal calls", "Only in debug builds, via assertions", "Nowhere: use assertions and document it"], a: 1, why: "<p>Validate what enters your module from outside, because you have no control over it. Inside the module you wrote both sides, so a null pointer is a bug to be fixed rather than a condition to handle.</p><p>Checking the same pointer at nine layers costs flash and cycles, and worse, it is noise that makes the checks that matter harder to see. A reader cannot tell which checks encode a real contract.</p><p>A defensible middle position is a runtime check at the boundary and an assert internally, so internal violations are loud during development and cost nothing in release.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-errors", d: 3, q: "Why count silent failures such as bus retries rather than ignoring them?", o: ["So performance can be tuned against them", "It makes an invisible degradation readable", "To satisfy MISRA's diagnostics rules", "So the watchdog can be triggered on them"], a: 1, why: "<p>A retry that succeeds produces correct behaviour, so nothing appears wrong. But a link retrying on one transfer in ten is much closer to failure than one retrying on one in ten thousand, and from outside they are indistinguishable.</p><p>A counter that a diagnostic command can read turns that into a number. A support engineer can compare a misbehaving unit against a healthy one and see the difference immediately.</p><p>The general principle is that anything the firmware recovers from silently is information you have chosen to destroy. Counting is cheap: a uint32_t and an increment.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-errors", d: 2, q: "Why give every wait a timeout?", o: ["To save power while the wait is pending", "An unbounded wait is a hang waiting to happen", "It is faster than polling without one", "The compiler warns about every loop without one"], a: 1, why: "<p>while (!(reg &amp; READY)) {} is correct until the device does not become ready, at which point the system stops with no diagnostic and no recovery.</p><p>A bounded wait converts that permanent hang into a returned error. The caller can retry, reset the device, report it as absent, or let the watchdog handle it, and all of those are better than stopping.</p><p>The same applies to queue receives, semaphore takes and DMA completions. Anywhere the code waits on something outside itself, ask what happens if that thing never arrives, because eventually it will not.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-errors", d: 3, q: "Why prefer failing loudly at init over degrading silently at run time?", o: ["It is easier to code than a fallback path", "A device reporting absent is a support call", "Initialisation is faster if you do not retry", "It uses less flash than degraded operation"], a: 1, why: "<p>A failure at init happens once, at a known moment, with the full context available and nothing yet depending on the result. It is the cheapest possible place to fail.</p><p>Silent degradation produces data that is wrong but plausible, which nobody can distinguish from correct without independent verification. It is discovered long after it started, often by a customer, and every measurement taken since is suspect.</p><p>This is the argument for verifying identity at init rather than assuming it, and for returning an error rather than substituting a default value. Loud and early beats quiet and late every time.</p>" },

// ---------------------------------------------------------- emb-buses
{ t: "emb-buses", d: 2, q: "The datasheet says 7-bit address 0x68 and the analyser decodes 0x34. What happened?", o: ["The device is faulty and NAKs everything", "The address was not shifted left for the wire", "The bus speed is wrong, so bits are misread", "The pull-ups are too weak for the bus capacitance"], a: 1, why: "<p>I2C transmits the seven address bits in the top seven positions of the first byte, with the read/write bit in bit 0. So the wire carries 0x68 &lt;&lt; 1, which is 0xD0 for a write.</p><p>An analyser decoding the raw byte and reporting the top seven bits of an unshifted 0x68 sees 0x34, exactly half.</p><p>The decoded address being precisely half the datasheet value is the signature of this fault, and it is worth recognising instantly. Note that some APIs take the 7-bit address and shift internally while others take the pre-shifted byte, which is why the mistake is so common.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-buses", d: 1, q: "In I2C, what defines a START condition?", o: ["SCL falling while SDA is held high", "SDA falling while SCL is high", "Both lines going low together", "Nine clock pulses"], a: 1, why: "<p>The protocol reserves this specific pattern. During data transfer, SDA is only permitted to change while SCL is low, so a transition on SDA while SCL is high cannot be data.</p><p>That makes it unambiguous: a falling SDA with SCL high is START, and a rising SDA with SCL high is STOP. Nothing else in the protocol produces those edges.</p><p>Knowing this lets you read a raw capture without relying on the analyser's decoder, which matters when the decoder is confused because the framing itself is broken.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-buses", d: 2, q: "Why must a register read use a repeated START rather than a STOP between phases?", o: ["It is faster than issuing a STOP each time", "A STOP lets the device reset its pointer", "A STOP mid-transaction is illegal on I2C", "It saves power by shortening the transfer"], a: 1, why: "<p>A register read is two phases: write the register number to set the device's internal pointer, then read from wherever that pointer sits.</p><p>A STOP ends the transaction. Many devices reset or advance their pointer on a STOP, so the following read comes from the wrong register. Worse, the bus is released, so another master or another task can address the same device and move the pointer in between.</p><p>A repeated START issues a new START without an intervening STOP, so the bus is never released and the two phases are one indivisible transaction. This is why a driver should use the combined transfer API rather than two separate calls.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-buses", d: 2, q: "What does a NAK on the address byte mean?", o: ["The device is busy and will answer later", "Nothing at that address acknowledged", "The data byte was corrupted in transit", "The bus is stuck with SDA held low"], a: 1, why: "<p>After clocking out eight bits the master releases SDA and clocks a ninth time. A device that recognised its address pulls SDA low during that clock, which is the ACK. If nobody pulls it low, the pull-up holds it high and the master reads a NAK.</p><p>So an address NAK means nothing responded. The causes are the wrong address, including the shift mistake, the part not fitted or unpowered, a wiring fault, or the device held in reset.</p><p>It specifically does not mean corruption, because there is no data yet. Distinguishing an address NAK from a data NAK narrows the search considerably.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-buses", d: 3, q: "Standard-mode I2C allows what maximum rise time?", o: ["100 ns", "300 ns", "1000 ns", "10 microseconds"], a: 2, why: "<p>Standard mode at 100 kHz allows 1000 ns; fast mode at 400 kHz tightens it to 300 ns.</p><p>The number matters because it lets you check a design on paper. Rise time is roughly 0.8 times R times C. An MCU's internal pull-up of around 45k against a realistic 100 pF of bus capacitance gives about 3.6 microseconds, which is several times outside even the relaxed limit.</p><p>That is the arithmetic behind the rule that internal pull-ups are not adequate for I2C. External 4.7k gives about 375 ns, fine for standard mode, and 2.2k gives about 176 ns, which meets fast mode.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-buses", d: 2, q: "To speed up a slow I2C rise time you should:", o: ["Increase the pull-up resistance", "Decrease the pull-up resistance", "Increase the bus clock speed", "Add capacitance to the bus lines"], a: 1, why: "<p>The line is pulled high passively through the resistor charging the bus capacitance, so the time constant is RC. Reducing R charges it faster.</p><p>The word 'stronger' for a pull-up means a lower resistance, which is worth getting straight because the language points the wrong way.</p><p>There is a lower bound. When a device pulls the line low it must sink the current the pull-up supplies, and I2C specifies 3 mA. At 3.3 V that puts the floor around 1.1k. Below that you exceed what the devices can sink and the low level rises out of spec.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-buses", d: 2, q: "SPI returns 0xFF for every byte. What does that indicate?", o: ["The device genuinely returns all ones", "Nothing is driving MISO at all", "The clock is too fast for the device", "The SPI mode is wrong for this part"], a: 1, why: "<p>An undriven line floats to whatever the pull-up or leakage takes it to, which is usually high, so every bit reads as 1.</p><p>The candidates are: chip select never asserted or asserted on the wrong pin, MISO not connected, the device unpowered or held in reset, or the wrong device selected entirely.</p><p>Contrast with reading all 0x00, which means something is actively holding the line low. That is a different fault, pointing at a short to ground, a device driving continuously, or a bus conflict. Distinguishing the two is a free halving of the search space.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-buses", d: 2, q: "A SPI device ID reads 0x82 instead of 0x41. What is wrong?", o: ["The wrong chip select line is asserted", "Clock phase: every bit arrives one out", "The MISO line is broken, so it floats high", "The device is still held in reset"], a: 1, why: "<p>0x41 is 0100 0001. 0x82 is 1000 0010, which is the same pattern shifted left by one. Every bit arrived one clock position out.</p><p>That is CPHA: the master is sampling on the wrong clock edge, so it captures each bit one edge early or late.</p><p>The point of the question is the method rather than the answer. Recognising the relationship between expected and observed, a shift, an inversion, a byte reversal, tells you which parameter is wrong. Trying all four SPI modes until one works finds the answer without teaching you anything, and leaves you unable to explain it.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-buses", d: 1, q: "During a SPI register read, MOSI going quiet after the address byte is:", o: ["A fault", "Correct behaviour", "A sign of wrong mode", "A clock problem"], a: 1, why: "<p>SPI is full duplex, so both lines carry data on every clock. But during the read phase the master has nothing left to send: it is only clocking to shift data out of the device.</p><p>So MOSI idles, usually low or held at whatever the last bit was, while MISO carries the response. That is exactly what should happen.</p><p>The reason this is worth knowing is that half of diagnosis is recognising correct behaviour. Time spent investigating a normal trace is time not spent on the actual fault, and unfamiliar traces invite exactly that.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-buses", d: 3, q: "A UART is clean at 9600 and garbage at 115200, consistently. Why does the low rate work?", o: ["There is less noise pickup at low speed", "A larger divider makes rounding cost less", "Fewer bits are sent, so fewer are wrong", "The receiver oversamples more at 9600"], a: 1, why: "<p>The baud rate comes from dividing a clock by an integer. At 9600 from a 16 MHz clock the divider is about 104, and rounding to 104 costs roughly 0.2 per cent. At 115200 the divider is about 8.7, and rounding to 9 costs about 3.5 per cent.</p><p>UART tolerates only around two to three per cent total across both ends, because the receiver samples the middle of each bit and accumulated error walks the sampling point off the last bit of the frame.</p><p>Add an RC oscillator's one to two per cent inaccuracy and you are past the budget at high rates while comfortably inside it at low ones. The word 'consistently' is the clue: systematic timing rather than noise, which would be intermittent.</p>" },

// ---------------------------------------------------------- emb-diagnose
{ t: "emb-diagnose", d: 2, q: "A valid analyser trace carries wrong data. What does that rule out?", o: ["A software fault above the driver", "A fault on the bus itself", "A bug in the bus driver", "Nothing: it rules nothing out"], a: 1, why: "<p>If the START, address, ACKs, data framing and STOP are all correct, the bus is doing its job. The wire faithfully transported what it was handed.</p><p>So the bytes were already wrong before transmission, which places the fault in software above the bus layer: the wrong buffer, the wrong values, or a shared library buffer overwritten by another context.</p><p>Most engineers reach for the analyser, see a clean trace, and get stuck because they were looking for a fault rather than for information. Knowing what a clean trace eliminates is what makes the instrument useful.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-diagnose", d: 2, q: "The I2C bus is dead after a debugger halt, and power-cycling the MCU does not help. Why?", o: ["The I2C peripheral has latched up", "A slave holds SDA low and needs clocks", "The pull-up resistors have failed", "The bus speed is now wrong after the halt"], a: 1, why: "<p>Halting the master mid-transaction leaves a slave partway through shifting out a byte. It is holding SDA low waiting for the next clock that will never come.</p><p>Resetting the MCU does nothing, because the slave is a separate chip that never saw the reset and is still in that state.</p><p>The recovery is to bit-bang up to nine clock pulses on SCL, letting the slave finish its byte and release SDA, then issue a STOP. Many drivers do this at init for exactly this reason. The alternative is power-cycling the whole board so the slave resets too.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-diagnose", d: 2, q: "How many clock pulses does I2C bus recovery need?", o: ["One", "Eight", "Up to nine", "Sixteen, one per bit plus ACK"], a: 2, why: "<p>Eight data bits plus the ACK bit makes nine positions. The slave could have been stalled at any one of them, so nine clocks guarantees it completes whatever byte it was in and releases SDA.</p><p>The recovery sequence is: check whether SDA is stuck low, and if so toggle SCL up to nine times, checking after each whether SDA has gone high, then generate a STOP so every device returns to idle.</p><p>Putting this in your init means a board that was halted under the debugger recovers on the next run instead of needing a power cycle, which saves a surprising amount of time during bring-up.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-diagnose", d: 2, q: "Why can a stuck I2C bus not recover by itself?", o: ["The master gives up after a retry limit", "A STOP needs SDA high, and it is held low", "The pull-ups are too weak to raise SDA", "The clock stops when the master halts"], a: 1, why: "<p>A STOP is defined as SDA rising while SCL is high. The master cannot force SDA high: it is an open-drain bus, so devices can only pull down and the pull-up does the rest.</p><p>With the slave holding SDA low, the master cannot generate the one condition that would end the transaction and return everyone to idle.</p><p>It is a genuine deadlock and it will persist indefinitely. The only exits are clocking the slave until it voluntarily releases the line, or removing power from the slave.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-diagnose", d: 1, q: "A board resets repeatedly. What do you read first?", o: ["The stack high water mark", "The reset reason register", "The supply rail on a scope", "The linker map"], a: 1, why: "<p>The part already recorded why it reset. One register read distinguishes power-on, brownout, watchdog, software reset, external pin and, on many parts, a fault.</p><p>Those point at completely different investigations: brownout means the power supply, watchdog means something stopped feeding it, fault means a memory access problem. Choosing between them before instrumenting anything saves hours.</p><p>This is why firmware should log the reset reason, along with the firmware version and build timestamp, at every boot. It converts a customer's vague report into evidence, and it costs a few lines.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-diagnose", d: 2, q: "Consistent wrong characters on a UART versus random corruption. What does the difference tell you?", o: ["Nothing useful; the two look identical", "Consistent means timing; random means noise", "Consistent means noise picked up along the line", "Both point to a broken or overly long cable"], a: 1, why: "<p>Consistent corruption means a deterministic cause: a baud rate mismatch, wrong frame format, wrong parity, or the wrong number of stop bits. The same input always produces the same wrong output.</p><p>Random corruption means something non-deterministic: electrical noise, a marginal ground, an overrun because the receiver was too slow, or a floating line.</p><p>The adjective in the original complaint is evidence, and it halves the search before you touch an instrument. It is also a reason to ask 'always, or sometimes?' as the first question about any reported fault.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-diagnose", d: 2, q: "Rising edges on SDA look like exponential curves rather than square corners. What does that mean?", o: ["The bus clock is too fast for this slave device", "The pull-up is too weak for the capacitance", "The device sitting on the bus is faulty", "The ground return path is disconnected"], a: 1, why: "<p>That curve is the RC charging characteristic of the pull-up resistor against the bus capacitance. It is the shape you get whenever a line is driven low actively and released to rise passively.</p><p>Falling edges will look sharp by comparison, because those are driven. The asymmetry is the giveaway that you are looking at an open-drain bus rather than a push-pull one.</p><p>The fixes are a lower pull-up resistance, a lower bus speed, fewer devices, or shorter wiring. Measure the rise time against the 1000 ns standard-mode or 300 ns fast-mode limit rather than judging by eye.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-diagnose", d: 3, q: "Why read a device's ID register before configuring it?", o: ["Most bus protocols require it first", "Cheapest proof that wiring and mode are right", "Reading the ID resets the device to its defaults", "It selects which register page is active"], a: 1, why: "<p>One transaction with a known expected answer proves an enormous amount at once: the device is present and powered, the address is right, the bus is electrically sound, the mode and clock phase are correct, and your read path works.</p><p>Nothing else gives you that much for one transfer, because you know in advance exactly what the answer should be.</p><p>Skip it and a wiring fault presents later as a configuration bug, with several writes already sent to a device that may not be there. That sends you to the wrong bench. It also produces a distinct error, wrong part rather than bus failure, which is genuinely new information the bus layer could not have supplied.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-diagnose", d: 2, q: "What is the correct first step when bringing up an unknown board?", o: ["Flash the vendor demo and see what happens", "Attach the debugger and halt the core", "Current-limit the supply, measure every rail", "Scope the crystal to check it oscillates"], a: 2, why: "<p>Power first, always. Current-limit the bench supply so a short does not destroy anything, apply power, and check that the current draw is roughly what you expected before looking at anything else.</p><p>Then measure every rail at its own test point rather than at the regulator, because a rail can be correct at the source and sagging at the load.</p><p>A board that browns out under load produces faults that look exactly like software bugs: random resets, corrupted data, peripherals that half work. Ruling that out first means every later failure is genuinely a firmware question.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-diagnose", d: 2, q: "Why build your own blinky rather than running the vendor demo?", o: ["The vendor demo is generally unreliable", "It proves toolchain, linker, startup and clock", "It is much faster to write than to go and download one", "The vendor demo needs a paid licence"], a: 1, why: "<p>A blinking LED from your own project proves four things at once: the toolchain produces a working binary, the linker script places it correctly, the startup code sets up the C environment, and the clock is configured well enough to run.</p><p>The vendor demo proves those too, but it hides all of them inside a project you did not write and may not be able to modify or reproduce.</p><p>The real value is ownership. Once your own build blinks a pin, every subsequent failure is in code you control and can inspect. Starting from the vendor project means the first hard problem sends you into someone else's build system.</p>" },

// ---------------------------------------------------------- emb-drivers
{ t: "emb-drivers", d: 2, q: "What is the strongest test that a driver's layering is correct?", o: ["It contains no global variables at all", "It compiles with no vendor SDK installed", "It is under five hundred lines of code", "It uses only fixed-width integer types"], a: 1, why: "<p>It is a binary, checkable question rather than a judgement. Either the driver's translation unit compiles against nothing but stdint.h, stddef.h and its own headers, or it does not.</p><p>If it does not, a platform dependency has leaked in, and with it goes off-target testing, portability to the next project, and the ability to exercise error paths.</p><p>The three other options are reasonable habits but none of them is decisive. A driver can have no globals and still call HAL_I2C_Master_Transmit, at which point it can only run on that vendor's parts with the sensor attached.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-drivers", d: 2, q: "Why does an injected transport keep the register number separate from the buffer?", o: ["It is more efficient on the physical wire itself", "The adapter decides how it goes on the bus", "It reduces the stack the call needs", "C requires the two to be separate"], a: 1, why: "<p>A register read looks completely different on the two buses. On I2C it is a write of the register number, a repeated START, then a read. On SPI it is a chip select, a command byte with a read bit set, then clocking out the data.</p><p>If the driver assembled the bytes itself it would be committed to one of those. By passing the register number as a separate parameter it states the intent and leaves the framing to the adapter.</p><p>That is why one driver can serve a part available in both I2C and SPI variants, which is common, and why the same driver survives moving to a different MCU.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-drivers", d: 3, q: "Why does an injected transport struct include a delay function?", o: ["For symmetry with the read and write members above", "Timing is platform-specific, and tests fake it", "Because I2C requires inter-byte delays", "So the driver can measure bus latency"], a: 1, why: "<p>A driver that calls vTaskDelay depends on FreeRTOS. One that calls HAL_Delay depends on the ST HAL. Either way the platform independence you built with the read and write functions is gone.</p><p>The test argument is the sharper one. To test that init times out when a reset bit never clears, you need the driver to believe several seconds have passed. With an injected delay the test double simply returns immediately and counts, so the test runs in microseconds.</p><p>With a real delay that single test takes the full timeout, and a suite of them becomes slow enough that people stop running it.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-drivers", d: 2, q: "What is the void *ctx in a transport struct for?", o: ["Somewhere for the adapter to report an error", "Whatever the adapter needs, passed back", "To guarantee the struct's alignment", "To carry debug information for the log"], a: 1, why: "<p>The driver never inspects it. It stores the pointer at init and hands it back as the first argument of every transport call.</p><p>The adapter puts whatever it needs in there: the I2C port handle, the device address, a chip select pin. Without it the adapter would need globals, which limits you to exactly one device of that type.</p><p>It is the same mechanism as the user pointer on a callback, and the C idiom for a closure. Two BNO055s on different buses become two driver instances with two contexts, and no code changes anywhere.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-drivers", d: 2, q: "Why keep the register map header out of the public include directory?", o: ["It speeds up compilation of the app", "So app code cannot bypass the driver", "It reduces the size of the binary", "C requires private headers to be hidden"], a: 1, why: "<p>If bno055_regs.h sits next to bno055.c rather than in include/, nothing outside the component can include it, because the addresses are not on anyone else's include path.</p><p>That matters because the moment application code can write a register directly, the driver stops being the single owner of the device. Its internal state no longer reflects reality, and a change to the driver can break code it does not know exists.</p><p>The point is that the build system enforces it rather than a comment asking people to behave. Encapsulation that depends on discipline erodes; encapsulation that produces a compile error does not.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-drivers", d: 2, q: "What is the benefit of an opaque device handle?", o: ["It produces smaller code in the final firmware", "Internals change without recompiling users", "Access through it is faster than a struct", "It removes the need for malloc entirely"], a: 1, why: "<p>Declaring typedef struct bno055_dev bno055_dev_t; in the header without defining the struct means the compiler does not know its size or members. Callers can hold a pointer and pass it around, and nothing else.</p><p>So you can add a field, reorder members, or change the calibration cache entirely, and no user code needs recompiling, let alone changing.</p><p>The cost is that callers cannot allocate one on the stack, since the size is unknown, so the driver must allocate it or provide a separate sized-buffer API. On a system that forbids dynamic allocation after init, the usual answer is a static pool inside the driver.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-drivers", d: 3, q: "A datasheet gives a maximum reset time of 5 ms. What should init do?", o: ["Delay 5 ms, exactly as the datasheet says", "Delay 10 ms, for a factor of two margin", "Poll the self-clearing bit, with a timeout", "Delay 1 ms and retry until it responds"], a: 2, why: "<p>A maximum is a bound under specified conditions, not a duration to wait. Polling the bit that the device clears when it is ready is both faster in the normal case and correct in the abnormal one.</p><p>It survives the part being slower than spec at temperature, the delay being optimised away, and the clock changing so the delay is no longer what it claims.</p><p>The timeout is what makes it safe: without one you have converted a bounded wait into a hang. And a timeout expiring is a real diagnostic, the part is not responding, which a fixed delay would have hidden by simply carrying on with a device that was not ready.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-drivers", d: 2, q: "Which four layers should a well-structured driver stack have?", o: ["HAL, driver, middleware, application", "Board, bus tools, device driver, application", "Startup, driver, RTOS, application", "Register map, driver, tests, application layer"], a: 1, why: "<p>Board knows the physical facts: which pins, which port, which address. Bus tools know the protocol and the vendor API. The device driver knows the register map and the device's behaviour. The application knows what it wants and nothing about how.</p><p>Each layer knows only what it must, which is what lets you swap any one of them. Change MCU: rewrite the board and bus layers. Change sensor: rewrite the driver. Change product: rewrite the application.</p><p>Answer 1 is the vendor's framing and it collapses the board and bus concerns into the HAL, which is exactly the coupling that stops drivers being portable or testable.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-drivers", d: 2, q: "Why should init verify identity rather than assume it?", o: ["The I2C specification requires it before config", "It separates a wrong part from a bus fault", "Reading the ID resets the device", "It makes the boot sequence faster"], a: 1, why: "<p>Those two failures send an engineer to different benches. A bus fault means wiring, pull-ups, address, or a stuck slave. A wrong chip ID means the part is not what the schematic says, or a variant with a different identity was fitted.</p><p>The check costs one transaction and has a known expected answer, so it proves the whole path at once.</p><p>Doing it before configuration matters because writes to a device that is not there, or is a different part, produce confusing partial behaviour. Failing at identity is clean; failing halfway through configuration is not.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-drivers", d: 3, q: "You port a driver whose vendor register map is unpublished. What is the professional handling?", o: ["Pretend the writes are fully understood", "Refuse the work until it is published", "Mark them, name the source, tell the client", "Reverse engineer the map before delivering it"], a: 2, why: "<p>Working with vendor code of unknown quality is completely normal, particularly in contract firmware. Undocumented magic register writes appear in reference drivers all the time.</p><p>What is not acceptable is letting a client believe the whole thing is understood. Mark the block, cite which vendor file and version it came from, and say plainly which parts you cannot explain.</p><p>Refusing the work is disproportionate and reverse engineering it may be neither possible nor billable. The professional position is that the risk is documented and visible, so the client can decide whether to accept it. That is a good answer to give in an interview, because it shows you distinguish technical uncertainty from concealment.</p>" },

// ---------------------------------------------------------- emb-testing
{ t: "emb-testing", d: 2, q: "Why is testability called a design property rather than a testing activity?", o: ["Because the tests are written first", "A driver calling the HAL only runs on target", "Because it needs a framework in place", "It is not; it is purely a test activity"], a: 1, why: "<p>Whether you can test a driver at all is decided when you choose how it reaches hardware, long before anyone writes a test.</p><p>Call the HAL directly and the code is welded to that vendor and that silicon. No host build, no error path exercised without provoking real faults, no test that runs when the board goes back to the client.</p><p>Retrofitting the indirection later is close to a rewrite, because every call site changes and the structure that made it untestable is usually load-bearing. That is why 'we will add tests later' so rarely happens: the obstacle is not effort, it is architecture.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-testing", d: 2, q: "What is a spy, as distinct from a stub?", o: ["A test that runs against real hardware", "A double that records what happened", "A double that returns fixed values", "A mock carrying preset expectations"], a: 1, why: "<p>A stub answers questions: it returns whatever canned value keeps the code under test running. It has no memory.</p><p>A spy keeps a log. Every call, its arguments and its order are recorded, so afterwards the test can assert that init wrote CTRL1 with 0x38 after the reset completed, rather than merely that init returned success.</p><p>The distinction matters because interaction is often the thing you care about in a driver. Grenning's four doubles are stub, spy, fake and mock, and being able to name which one a test needs is a good sign of having thought about what you are actually verifying.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-testing", d: 2, q: "What is a fake?", o: ["A test that is written to always report success", "A working but simplified implementation", "A stub with a timer driving its returns", "A mocked interrupt handler in the suite"], a: 1, why: "<p>A fake behaves like the real thing, just simply. A 256-byte array standing in for a device's register file responds to reads and writes coherently: write 0x38 to register 0x3D and reading it back gives 0x38.</p><p>That lets you exercise real driver logic, including sequences where a later step depends on an earlier write, which a stub returning fixed values cannot do.</p><p>You can also make a fake do things hardware will not: return the wrong chip ID, refuse to clear the reset bit, NAK the third write. That is how error paths get tested at all.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-testing", d: 3, q: "Which of these can only be tested with a double, not on real hardware?", o: ["That a good reading comes back correctly formed", "That a wrong chip ID gives a distinct error", "That the bus actually runs at 100 kHz", "That the part draws about 12 mA"], a: 1, why: "<p>You cannot make a real BNO055 report that it is a different part. Nor can you make it NAK the third write, or refuse to leave reset, without physically damaging it.</p><p>Yet those are exactly the paths that run when something is wrong in the field, which makes them the paths most worth testing and the ones most likely never to have executed.</p><p>The other three options are the reverse: bus speed and current draw are electrical properties that only a real board can answer, and a good reading is testable both ways. Knowing which tier answers which question is the skill.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-testing", d: 2, q: "Why watch each test fail before trusting it?", o: ["To time how long each test takes to run", "One that never failed may assert nothing", "TDD's red-green-refactor requires it", "To check the compiler optimised it"], a: 1, why: "<p>A test that passes tells you either that the code is correct or that the test is not checking anything. Those look identical from the outside.</p><p>The failure modes are common and quiet: an assertion with inverted logic, a test body that never runs because of a typo in the name or a guard, a fixture that returns success unconditionally, an assertion accidentally deleted.</p><p>Seeing it fail for the expected reason, then pass after the fix, is the only cheap evidence that it is connected to the behaviour it claims to check. It is the reason the red step exists in TDD, and it is worth doing even when you write the test after the code.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-testing", d: 2, q: "You find yourself stubbing the entire I2C API to build a driver test. What does that tell you?", o: ["The test you are attempting is far too ambitious", "The layering is wrong: still platform-coupled", "You need a proper mocking framework here", "Nothing: that is normal for any driver"], a: 1, why: "<p>The amount you have to stub is a direct measurement of coupling. A properly layered driver needs three functions replaced: read, write and delay.</p><p>Having to fake the whole vendor I2C API, with its handles, init structures and status enums, means the driver is talking to the platform rather than to an abstraction you defined.</p><p>The useful move is to treat the difficulty of the test as a design signal rather than a problem to push through. Painful setup usually means the code is telling you something about its structure, and fixing the structure is cheaper than maintaining the elaborate stub.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-testing", d: 1, q: "What should a test name communicate?", o: ["The name of the function that is under test here", "The behaviour promised, readable on its own", "The author, and the date it was added", "The bug number it was first written for"], a: 1, why: "<p>test_init_returns_wrong_part_when_chip_id_mismatches states a promise. Reading the list of test names tells you what the driver guarantees, without opening a single implementation file.</p><p>test_init_2 tells you nothing, and when it fails you have to read the body to find out what broke.</p><p>The suite becomes documentation that cannot go stale, because a description that stops being true also stops passing. That is worth more than any comment, and it costs only the effort of a longer name.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-testing", d: 2, q: "Why start with plain assertions and a main() rather than a framework?", o: ["Frameworks are unreliable on embedded", "Setting up infrastructure eats the whole day", "Frameworks do not work on a cross build", "It is required before you can add any framework"], a: 1, why: "<p>The first goal is to prove the driver can be compiled and exercised off-target at all. A main() with a few if statements and a return code does that in ten minutes.</p><p>Setting up Ceedling, a mocking framework, a build integration and a runner generator before that point means a day spent on infrastructure with nothing proven, and if the driver turns out to be too coupled to build on the host, all of it was wasted.</p><p>Unity, CMock and Ceedling are genuinely worth adopting, and the migration is easy once the tests exist. Order matters more than the choice.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-testing", d: 3, q: "Which assertion needs a spy rather than a fake?", o: ["That a read returns the value 0xA0", "That init wrote CTRL1 after the reset", "That an error comes back on a NAK", "That the returned handle is not null"], a: 1, why: "<p>Ordering is a property of the interaction, not of the final state. A fake ends up with the right value in its register array regardless of what order the writes happened in, so it cannot distinguish the two.</p><p>A spy records the sequence, so the test can assert that the write to CTRL1 appears after the reset-complete poll.</p><p>This matters in real drivers because many devices require configuration in a specific order, and a driver that happens to work on your bench may fail on a slower part where the ordering becomes visible.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-testing", d: 2, q: "Why is a host test suite valuable eighteen months after a project ends?", o: ["It is not: the code will have moved on by then", "It runs when the hardware is long gone", "It documents how the build is done", "It reduces the shipped binary size"], a: 1, why: "<p>In contract work the hardware is not yours. There may be one board, shared between engineers, and it goes back at the end of the engagement.</p><p>Any test that needs it attached stops working that day, taking your regression coverage with it. When the client returns with a bug eighteen months later, you have source and nothing to run.</p><p>A host suite still runs on a laptop, so you can reproduce, fix and verify before anyone ships a board back to you. That is a commercial argument as much as a technical one, and it is worth making in those terms.</p>" },

// ---------------------------------------------------------- emb-hil
{ t: "emb-hil", d: 2, q: "Which question belongs to a host unit test rather than a HIL rig?", o: ["Does the driver time out on a stuck bit", "What is the interrupt latency under heavy load", "Does the board brown out at 2.9 V", "Is the I2C rise time within spec"], a: 0, why: "<p>A reset that never completes is pure logic. A fake simply never sets the bit, the injected delay advances time instantly, and the test proves the timeout path in microseconds with no hardware.</p><p>The other three are physical: latency needs a real core under real load, brownout needs a programmable supply, rise time needs a scope on a real bus.</p><p>Getting the tiers right is the whole point. Every test that can be pushed down to the host runs on every commit, needs nothing scarce, and survives the hardware leaving.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-hil", d: 2, q: "What limits the value of a HIL rig?", o: ["The number of instruments on the bench", "The quality of the requirements it tests", "The speed of the PC driving the rig", "The debug probe's throughput"], a: 1, why: "<p>A rig executes scenarios and reports pass or fail. It has no opinion about which scenarios matter.</p><p>Point it at vague requirements and you get a green report while the product misbehaves, because nothing in the suite corresponds to the thing that is wrong.</p><p>This is why the requirement question and the HIL question are really the same question. 'The system shall be responsive' cannot be automated. 'LED duty cycle shall be proportional to the quality register, updated at 20 Hz' becomes a test someone can write, and a failure someone can act on.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-hil", d: 2, q: "Which fault can only be injected with hardware?", o: ["A wrong chip ID coming from the device", "A NAK on the address byte", "Collapsing the supply mid-write", "An invalid argument to the API"], a: 2, why: "<p>A programmable supply can drop the rail to 2.7 V during a flash write and hold it there, repeatedly and reproducibly. Nothing in software reproduces what that does to a part.</p><p>The first two are trivially faked: a stub returns the wrong ID, a fake returns a NAK on the third transfer. An invalid argument is just a parameter.</p><p>The general division is that logic and protocol faults belong to the host tier, while electrical, timing and marginal-condition faults need instruments. Brownout during a write is the archetypal example because it is both realistic and destructive to firmware that assumed it could not happen.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-hil", d: 1, q: "Why push a test down to the host tier where possible?", o: ["Host tests are inherently more thorough", "It runs on every commit, with no hardware", "HIL rigs are unreliable in practice", "Regulations require host testing first"], a: 1, why: "<p>Speed and availability. A host test runs in milliseconds on any laptop, so it can run on every commit and give feedback while the change is still in your head.</p><p>A test needing scarce shared hardware runs when the hardware is free, which in practice means less often, and it stops running entirely the day the board goes back.</p><p>This is not an argument against HIL, which answers questions nothing else can. It is an argument for putting each test at the cheapest tier that can genuinely answer it, and being honest about which tier that is.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-hil", d: 3, q: "Which requirement is testable?", o: ["The LED shall be brighter when the quality is better", "The system shall be responsive to input", "LED duty shall track the quality register, 20 Hz", "The device shall be reliable in service"], a: 2, why: "<p>A testable requirement has a measurable quantity, a defined relationship and a number. You can read the quality register, measure the duty cycle, check proportionality, and time the updates.</p><p>'Brighter when quality is better' has a direction but no relationship or rate, so two implementations could both satisfy it and behave completely differently.</p><p>'Responsive' and 'reliable' cannot pass or fail. They are not requirements, they are aspirations, and the honest response when handed one is to ask what number would settle it. That conversation is usually the most valuable part of the requirements process.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-hil", d: 2, q: "What does a typical HIL rig consist of?", o: ["A second microcontroller driving the target", "A PC driving the target plus instruments", "A logic analyser on its own", "An in-circuit emulator and a script"], a: 1, why: "<p>A host script flashes the firmware through a debug probe, then drives the scenario: a programmable supply sets the rail, a signal generator provides stimulus, a meter and a scope take measurements, and the target under test does its job.</p><p>Everything is scripted over the instruments' comms ports, so the whole run is repeatable and produces a report rather than an engineer's recollection.</p><p>Bermondsey's BELIeVE product is exactly this, written in JavaScript. Understanding the shape of such a rig, and which questions it is uniquely able to answer, is directly relevant to that kind of role.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-hil", d: 2, q: "What does an overnight soak run find that a manual test does not?", o: ["Compile errors in rarely built code", "The once-a-day fault nobody can reproduce", "Stack overflows, and nothing else", "Nothing a manual test would not find"], a: 1, why: "<p>Rare faults need volume. A race that fires on one cycle in fifty thousand will never appear in a morning of manual testing, but it appears reliably in a run of a million cycles.</p><p>Slow leaks are the same: a handle not released on one error path is invisible until enough hours have passed.</p><p>Automation is what makes the volume affordable. The rig runs overnight for free while nobody is there, and the report in the morning is either clean or contains the reproduction you have been unable to get by hand, which is most of the work of fixing it.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-hil", d: 3, q: "Why does traceability from report to requirement number matter?", o: ["It makes the report look professional", "It is the evidence, and it exposes gaps", "It speeds up the rig's test selection", "It reduces the number of false failures seen"], a: 1, why: "<p>In regulated work, an auditor asks how you know the product meets a requirement, and a test report that names the requirement is the answer. Without the link, the testing may have been thorough and you cannot demonstrate it.</p><p>The underrated half is the reverse direction. Mapping tests to requirements shows you which requirements have no test, and that list is usually surprising.</p><p>It is worth doing even on unregulated projects for that reason alone. The traceability matrix is a coverage report in requirement space rather than code space, and it finds different gaps.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-hil", d: 2, q: "Which tier answers 'is this particular board good'?", o: ["Host unit tests", "HIL", "Bench bring-up", "Static analysis"], a: 2, why: "<p>That is a one-off question about one physical board, answered by hand in minutes: rails at their test points, current draw sane, clock running, chip IDs reading back, own blinky running.</p><p>Host tests say nothing about a specific board. HIL is automated regression against requirements, which is a different question and disproportionate for one board.</p><p>The part people skip is writing the results down. The known-good values, current draw, rail voltages, ID register contents, are what let you diagnose the second board a year later by comparison. Ten minutes of notes, enormous later value.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-hil", d: 2, q: "Why is client hardware described as a scheduling problem, not just a quality one?", o: ["It is expensive to buy a second one", "Work stops when the board goes back", "It needs calibrating before each use", "It is fragile and breaks in transit"], a: 1, why: "<p>In contract work there is often one board, shared between engineers, on loan for a fixed period. That makes hardware access a constraint on when work can happen, not just on how good the testing is.</p><p>Any logic that can only be exercised with the board attached blocks on availability, and stops being maintainable the day it is returned.</p><p>Framing it this way is useful in an interview because it shows you understand the commercial reality of the work rather than just the engineering ideal. It is also the strongest practical argument for the injected-transport design.</p>" },

// ---------------------------------------------------------- emb-defensive
{ t: "emb-defensive", d: 2, q: "What is the purpose of MISRA's deviation process?", o: ["To let inconvenient rules be ignored quietly", "To make departures visible and justified", "To speed up the certification process", "To replace testing with static rules"], a: 1, why: "<p>Every real codebase breaks some rules. A standard with no escape hatch is either ignored entirely or forces contortions that make the code worse.</p><p>The deviation process requires you to record which rule, where, why, and what alternative mitigation applies. That converts a violation from an accident nobody noticed into an argued engineering decision on the record.</p><p>The value is in the record rather than in the rule. A reviewer or auditor can then evaluate the reasoning, and the next engineer knows the departure was deliberate rather than an oversight to be tidied up.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-defensive", d: 1, q: "Which change usually gives the biggest defect reduction for the least effort?", o: ["Adopting a memory-safe language such as Rust", "-Wall -Wextra -Werror, and fixing it all", "Writing considerably more unit tests", "Buying a commercial static analyser"], a: 1, why: "<p>The compiler already detects several real defect classes: sizeof on a pointer parameter, uninitialised use, sign comparison problems, dead assignments, missing returns. Most codebases are simply not listening.</p><p>Turning warnings on costs an afternoon of cleanup. Turning them into errors is what makes it stick, because otherwise the count creeps back up and nobody notices the new one among the old.</p><p>Analysers and tests are both worth having, and both cost considerably more. Start with the tool you already have and are already ignoring.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-defensive", d: 2, q: "Why do embedded coding standards commonly ban recursion?", o: ["It is slower than an equivalent loop", "Worst-case stack use cannot be analysed", "Not every embedded compiler will support it", "It prevents the compiler from inlining"], a: 1, why: "<p>Static stack analysis works by walking the call graph and summing the deepest path. That requires the graph to be finite and acyclic.</p><p>Recursion makes depth depend on the data, so the tool cannot bound it and you cannot prove the stack will not overflow. On a part with 32 kB of RAM and no MMU, an overflow silently corrupts whatever is adjacent.</p><p>The ban is therefore about provability rather than performance. Where a recursive algorithm is the natural one, the usual answer is an explicit stack of bounded size, which makes the worst case visible and checkable.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-defensive", d: 2, q: "Which warning catches sizeof(buf) on an array parameter passed to memset?", o: ["-Wshadow", "-Wsizeof-pointer-memaccess", "-Wconversion, with -Wsign-conversion", "-Wundef"], a: 1, why: "<p>GCC recognises the specific pattern of sizeof applied to the same expression being passed as the destination of a memory function, and warns that you are almost certainly getting the pointer size.</p><p>It is enabled by -Wall, so it costs nothing to have. This is a concrete instance of the general point: the array-to-pointer decay bug is one the compiler already knows about.</p><p>It does not catch every case, since the sizeof might be computed elsewhere, which is why the real fix is an explicit length parameter on any function that writes into a caller's buffer.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-defensive", d: 2, q: "What does -Wundef catch?", o: ["A call to an undefined function", "An undefined identifier used in an #if", "A variable read before it is set", "Constructs that are undefined behaviour"], a: 1, why: "<p>The preprocessor treats an unknown identifier in an #if as 0 rather than as an error. So #if CONFIG_ENBALE_LOGGING with a typo is simply false, and the whole block disappears.</p><p>Nothing warns you by default. The code compiles, the feature is silently absent, and it can take a long time to work out why the logging you configured is not appearing.</p><p>-Wundef makes it a diagnostic. It is a small flag with a specific target, and it belongs in the default set alongside -Wall and -Wextra on any project with configuration macros.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-defensive", d: 3, q: "Why ban dynamic allocation after initialisation on many embedded projects?", o: ["malloc is too slow for a real-time path", "Worst-case memory use becomes unprovable", "malloc is not implemented on bare metal", "It fragments flash as well as RAM"], a: 1, why: "<p>Two problems. Fragmentation means a long-running system can have plenty of free memory in total and still fail to satisfy a request, and whether that happens depends on the history of allocations, which you cannot enumerate.</p><p>And an allocation failure can occur at any point, so every call site needs a recovery path, most of which are never tested.</p><p>Allocating everything at startup means failure happens once, at a predictable moment, before the system is doing anything, where it can be handled properly or reported cleanly. That turns an unbounded risk into a bounded one, which is the whole aim.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-defensive", d: 2, q: "What does static analysis find that a compiler typically does not?", o: ["Syntax errors the compiler misses", "Cross-function leaks and null dereferences", "Headers that are missing from a file", "Link errors, before the link step even runs"], a: 1, why: "<p>A compiler largely reasons within one function at a time. An analyser builds a model across call boundaries and along execution paths, so it can see that one error return out of five forgets to free a buffer.</p><p>That is where resource leaks live: not in the main path, which gets exercised constantly, but in the third error branch that has never run.</p><p>Analysers also track null-ness across functions, catching the case where a function that can return null is used without a check somewhere else entirely. Both classes are essentially invisible to review as well, because they require holding several files in your head at once.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-defensive", d: 1, q: "Why brace every if and loop body, even one-liners?", o: ["It is purely a matter of house style", "Adding a second statement later is a bug", "Braced code compiles marginally faster", "MISRA forbids single-statement bodies"], a: 1, why: "<p>The unbraced form is correct as written. It becomes wrong when someone adds a second line, indents it to match, and the compiler attaches it to nothing.</p><p>Apple's goto fail bug was exactly this: a duplicated goto line outside an unbraced if, which skipped the signature verification in TLS. It shipped and was a serious security failure.</p><p>Two characters remove the entire class. -Wmisleading-indentation catches many instances but not all, so the habit is worth having regardless of the flag.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-defensive", d: 2, q: "Why require a default case in every switch?", o: ["The compiler requires a default case here", "It forces a decision about odd values", "It makes the switch dispatch faster", "It reduces the generated code size"], a: 1, why: "<p>Without a default, an unhandled value falls straight through and the switch does nothing at all. That is a decision nobody made, and it is indistinguishable from correct behaviour.</p><p>The value could be unexpected because someone added an enum member and did not update this switch, or because memory was corrupted. Both are worth knowing about.</p><p>What the default should do depends: log, return an error, or treat it as a fault. The point is that it is chosen deliberately. Note the tension with -Wswitch-enum, which warns about missing enum cases only when there is no default, so some projects prefer no default on enum switches specifically to keep that warning working.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-defensive", d: 3, q: "What is the honest argument for a coding standard in an interview?", o: ["It guarantees the code will be correct", "The discipline and record, not the rules", "It removes the need for code review", "It is a legal requirement in the UK"], a: 1, why: "<p>Reciting rule numbers signals that you have read a document. Explaining what the rules target signals that you understand the problem they address.</p><p>The substance is that a standard removes a category of argument from code review, makes departures explicit rather than accidental, and gives a team one answer to questions that otherwise get relitigated.</p><p>Being able to say where you would deviate, and why, is stronger still, because it shows judgement rather than compliance. A candidate who says MISRA is valuable but that they would deviate on a specific rule with a recorded justification is more convincing than one who says it guarantees anything.</p>" },

// ---------------------------------------------------------- emb-rtos
{ t: "emb-rtos", d: 1, q: "What justifies creating a second task?", o: ["It makes the code tidier to read through", "A different rate, blocking, or priority", "Every driver deserves its own task", "It lowers total stack consumption"], a: 1, why: "<p>Those three are the real reasons. Something runs at 100 Hz while something else runs at 1 Hz. Something blocks on a queue or a bus and would otherwise stall everything. Something must preempt other work when it becomes ready.</p><p>Tasks are not free: each costs a stack, a control block, and a scheduling decision, and every pair of tasks that share anything introduces state you now have to protect.</p><p>'Tidier code' is the answer that leads to a system with eleven tasks and a concurrency bug nobody can find. Structure the code with functions and modules; use tasks when the timing genuinely differs.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-rtos", d: 2, q: "You use a binary semaphore to protect a shared resource. What do you lose?", o: ["Nothing at all: it behaves just like a mutex", "Priority inheritance, so inversion returns", "The ability to signal from an ISR", "Interrupt safety on the take path"], a: 1, why: "<p>A semaphore is a counter with no notion of ownership. Anyone can give it, including a task that never took it, and the kernel does not know which task is holding it.</p><p>A mutex has an owner, which is what makes priority inheritance possible: when a high-priority task blocks on it, the kernel can temporarily raise the holder's priority so it finishes and releases.</p><p>Without an owner there is nobody to promote, so a medium-priority task can preempt the holder indefinitely. The rule of thumb is mutex for mutual exclusion, semaphore for signalling, and the two are not interchangeable despite both being binary.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-rtos", d: 2, q: "In priority inversion, what actually blocks the high-priority task?", o: ["The mutex itself, which is never released at all", "A medium-priority task preempts the holder", "The scheduler's tick handler", "Interrupt latency in the driver"], a: 1, why: "<p>High blocks on a mutex held by Low, which is expected and bounded if Low simply finishes. The problem is Medium.</p><p>Medium is runnable, does not need the mutex, and outranks Low, so it preempts Low. Low cannot finish, so it cannot release the mutex, so High cannot run. High is now effectively waiting behind a task it has no relationship with, for an unbounded time.</p><p>That is why it is called inversion: the effective priority order becomes Medium above High. Priority inheritance fixes it by raising Low to High's priority while it holds the mutex, so Medium cannot preempt it.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-rtos", d: 3, q: "Which famous failure was caused by priority inversion?", o: ["Ariane 5", "Mars Pathfinder", "Therac-25", "Patriot missile timing"], a: 1, why: "<p>Pathfinder landed in 1997 and began resetting on the surface. A high-priority bus management task blocked on a mutex held by a low-priority meteorological task, which was being preempted by a medium-priority communications task. The watchdog noticed the bus task had not run and reset the spacecraft.</p><p>The fix was to enable priority inheritance on that mutex, uploaded from Earth to a vehicle on Mars.</p><p>The other three are also worth knowing but have different causes: Ariane 5 was an unhandled conversion overflow in reused code, Therac-25 a race combined with removed hardware interlocks, and the Patriot failure an accumulating fixed-point time error.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-rtos", d: 2, q: "How should a task stack be sized?", o: ["Use whatever default the RTOS happens to give", "Measure the high water mark, add headroom", "As large as the RAM budget allows", "Match the largest local in the task"], a: 1, why: "<p>uxTaskGetStackHighWaterMark reports the minimum free space that task has ever had, because FreeRTOS fills the stack with a pattern and counts how much remains untouched.</p><p>Run the system through its worst case, which usually includes a rarely taken path you have to provoke deliberately, read the number, and leave meaningful headroom.</p><p>Guessing goes wrong both ways. Too large wastes RAM you do not have, and since stacks come from the heap, it can starve later task creation. Too small corrupts adjacent memory with no error, producing a fault somewhere unrelated.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-rtos", d: 3, q: "FreeRTOS's stack size parameter is in words, but some ports take bytes. Why does that matter?", o: ["It does not matter; the port converts it", "Porting silently changes it fourfold", "It affects the task's priority band", "It changes the effective tick rate"], a: 1, why: "<p>xTaskCreate takes the depth in words, so 1024 means 4096 bytes on a 32-bit part. Some ports and wrappers, including ESP-IDF's xTaskCreate, take bytes instead.</p><p>Move code between them without noticing and a stack that was 4 kB becomes 1 kB, or the reverse. Nothing warns you.</p><p>The failure is the worst kind: memory corruption in unrelated data, appearing only when a deep code path runs. Always check the units in the port you are using rather than assuming, and confirm with the high water mark afterwards.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-rtos", d: 2, q: "Why prefer a queue over shared memory between tasks?", o: ["Queues are faster than taking a mutex", "It removes the shared state, not protects it", "Queues use less RAM than a buffer", "Shared memory is banned by most standards"], a: 1, why: "<p>A queue copies the data, so after the send the producer's copy and the consumer's copy are independent. There is no object that two tasks both hold references to.</p><p>That eliminates the class of bug rather than defending against it: no lock to forget, no ordering to get wrong, no partial update visible to the other side.</p><p>The cost is the copy, which matters only for large items, and there you can queue a pointer to a buffer from a pool along with an ownership convention. The general principle, one owner plus message passing, is the single most effective structural answer to concurrency problems.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-rtos", d: 2, q: "Why do RTOS APIs provide FromISR variants?", o: ["They are faster than the normal versions", "An ISR cannot block, so it never waits", "They use less stack in the handler", "They exist for backwards compatibility"], a: 1, why: "<p>The normal API can block, which is meaningless and dangerous in interrupt context. The FromISR variant returns immediately with a failure instead of waiting.</p><p>It also takes a pxHigherPriorityTaskWoken pointer. If the operation unblocked a task more important than the one that was interrupted, the flag is set and the handler calls portYIELD_FROM_ISR before returning, so the scheduler switches immediately rather than at the next tick.</p><p>Calling the blocking variant from an ISR is a classic fault and often corrupts kernel state silently rather than failing cleanly, which is why configASSERT is worth enabling during development.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-rtos", d: 2, q: "A task never runs. What are the two most likely causes?", o: ["A bad priority and a bad stack size", "Creation failed unchecked, or nothing blocks", "The wrong tick rate or the wrong queue", "The watchdog, or a brownout resetting the board"], a: 1, why: "<p>xTaskCreate allocates the stack from the heap and returns pdFAIL if it cannot. Ignore that return value and the task simply does not exist, with nothing to indicate it.</p><p>The other cause is starvation: a higher-priority task that busy-waits instead of blocking never yields, so nothing below it ever gets scheduled. FreeRTOS is strictly priority-based, so this is guaranteed rather than merely likely.</p><p>Check both by asserting on the create return, and by looking at whether every high-priority task has a genuine blocking call in its loop rather than a poll.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-rtos", d: 3, q: "Which symptom most suggests a task stack overflow?", o: ["A perfectly clean reset with no other symptom", "Corruption in unrelated data, under one path", "A watchdog timeout on that task", "Generally slow response to input"], a: 1, why: "<p>An overflow writes past the end of the stack into whatever is next in memory, which is often another task's control block or a static buffer.</p><p>So the visible fault has no obvious connection to the task at fault, and it only appears when the deep path runs, which may be a rare error branch.</p><p>Two things make it findable: enable configCHECK_FOR_STACK_OVERFLOW, which checks the pattern at every context switch and calls a hook, and check high water marks routinely rather than after a problem. The MPU can also be used to place a guard region, which turns silent corruption into an immediate fault.</p>" },

// ---------------------------------------------------------- emb-faults
{ t: "emb-faults", d: 2, q: "A task occasionally takes 3 seconds and the 2-second watchdog resets the board. Which fix is right?", o: ["Increase the watchdog timeout past 3 seconds", "Feed the watchdog inside the long operation", "Find and bound whatever takes 3 seconds", "Disable the watchdog on that path"], a: 2, why: "<p>Something normally taking milliseconds occasionally taking three seconds is the fault. The watchdog is doing its job by reporting it.</p><p>Every other option makes the watchdog worse at detecting a genuine hang: a longer timeout means slower recovery, feeding it inside the operation means it no longer proves the system is progressing, disabling it removes the protection entirely.</p><p>Find the cause. It is usually an unbounded wait, a retry loop with no limit, or a blocking call whose timeout is far larger than anyone realised. Bound it, then the watchdog goes back to being a last resort rather than a symptom.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-faults", d: 2, q: "Why is feeding the watchdog from a periodic timer callback wrong?", o: ["It costs CPU time on every single tick", "It runs regardless of system health", "Software timers are not reliable enough", "It cannot be done from an ISR context"], a: 1, why: "<p>The timer interrupt keeps firing whether or not any task is making progress. Deadlock every task in the system and the callback still runs, still feeds the watchdog, and the board never resets.</p><p>That is precisely the situation the watchdog was installed to catch, and you have made it invisible.</p><p>The correct pattern is that each monitored task sets a flag when it completes a cycle, and a supervisor feeds the watchdog only when every flag is set, clearing them afterwards. Then feeding it is evidence that the system is actually working.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-faults", d: 1, q: "What is a watchdog for?", o: ["Measuring how long each operation takes", "Catching a system that has stopped working", "Saving power between wake periods", "Detecting a brownout on the supply"], a: 1, why: "<p>It is a last resort: an independent timer that resets the part if the software stops proving it is alive. It exists for the failure you did not anticipate.</p><p>Keeping that single purpose in mind is what makes the other watchdog questions easy. Any change that makes it less likely to fire, a longer timeout, feeding it from a timer, feeding it inside a long operation, is making it worse at its only job.</p><p>It also explains why recording the reset reason matters: a watchdog reset that nobody logs is the system telling you something and nobody listening.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-faults", d: 2, q: "Which is NOT a common cause of a hard fault?", o: ["Null pointer dereference", "Unaligned access on Cortex-M0", "Accessing a peripheral whose clock is off", "An unhandled compiler warning"], a: 3, why: "<p>The first three all produce a genuine exception. A null dereference is an access to address 0, which on most parts is either unmapped or read-only. Cortex-M0 has no unaligned access support at all. And a peripheral with its clock gated off does not respond on the bus, producing a bus fault.</p><p>That third one is worth remembering, because it is the most common bring-up fault and it looks nothing like a memory error. Enable the clock before touching any register.</p><p>A compiler warning is a static diagnostic. It may point at code that will fault, but it is not itself a runtime exception.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-faults", d: 3, q: "What does a Cortex-M fault handler have available to diagnose the fault?", o: ["Nothing useful without a debugger", "The stacked PC and LR, plus fault status", "Only the reason code for the reset", "The full call stack, already unwound for you"], a: 1, why: "<p>On exception entry the core automatically stacks eight registers, including the PC at the point of the fault and the LR. The handler can locate that frame by examining the EXC_RETURN value in LR to decide whether the main or process stack was in use.</p><p>Alongside that, CFSR breaks the fault into memory, bus and usage categories with specific bits, and BFAR or MMFAR hold the offending address when the corresponding valid bit is set.</p><p>So you can recover the faulting instruction address and the address it tried to access, then decode both against the ELF. That turns 'it hard faults' into a file and line. A minimal handler that captures and prints these is worth writing once and reusing on every project.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-faults", d: 2, q: "Why keep the ELF for every firmware release?", o: ["It is what the programmer flashes", "Backtraces decode against its symbols", "It contains the linker script used", "It is needed for the licence audit"], a: 1, why: "<p>The image on the chip is stripped: no symbols, no line numbers, just instructions. A fault address from the field is a bare number.</p><p>The ELF from that exact build maps it to a function and a line via addr2line. Without it you have an address and nothing to resolve it against.</p><p>The trap is decoding against a different build. Optimisation moves code, so a slightly different binary produces a confident answer pointing at the wrong function, which is worse than no answer. Archive the ELF and map file with the release, alongside the toolchain version.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-faults", d: 2, q: "What should firmware record at every boot to make field faults diagnosable?", o: ["The uptime accumulated since last reset", "Reset reason, version and build stamp", "The stack pointer at entry to main", "The heap size and how much is free"], a: 1, why: "<p>Together they answer what happened and what was running. The reset reason distinguishes watchdog from brownout from fault from a normal power cycle. The version and build timestamp tell you which binary, which is often not the one you assumed.</p><p>It converts a customer report of 'it keeps restarting' into evidence you can act on before anyone travels to site.</p><p>It costs a few lines and a handful of bytes, and the version being wrong is a surprisingly common finding: field units frequently run something older than the team believes.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-faults", d: 3, q: "How do you make an intermittent fault easier to find?", o: ["Wait for longer, and gather many more logs", "Raise the rate: faster bus, more load", "Add logging and nothing else", "Reduce the optimisation level"], a: 1, why: "<p>If the cause is a race, the fault occurs when a narrow window is hit. Increasing the rate of the operations involved makes that window get hit more often, turning a weekly failure into a per-minute one.</p><p>Raise the bus speed, increase the sample rate, shorten the timeouts, add background load, run more tasks. Any of these can move the probability enough to make it reproducible.</p><p>Reproduction on demand is most of the work, because without it you cannot distinguish a fix from a coincidence. Note that reducing optimisation usually makes it less likely, which is why 'it goes away at -O0' is information rather than a solution.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-faults", d: 2, q: "Why expose counters for bus retries and dropped bytes?", o: ["They are useful when benchmarking the bus", "A silent degradation becomes readable", "The RTOS requires them for statistics", "They can be used to trip the watchdog"], a: 1, why: "<p>Recovery hides the problem. A retry that succeeds produces correct output, so a link on the edge of failure is indistinguishable from a healthy one until it crosses the line.</p><p>A counter makes the difference visible. Comparing a misbehaving unit against a working one immediately shows whether the link is marginal.</p><p>The cost is a uint32_t and an increment. The general habit is that anything the firmware silently recovers from is information you are discarding, and counting it is the cheapest possible way to keep it.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-faults", d: 3, q: "Proving you have fixed an intermittent fault is hard because:", o: ["The fix may itself introduce a bug", "Absence of a symptom is weak evidence", "Compilers vary between the builds", "The watchdog may still be masking the fault"], a: 1, why: "<p>A fault appearing once a day does not reappear during an afternoon of testing whether or not you fixed it. So 'it has not happened since' means very little.</p><p>Worse, many changes alter timing incidentally, so a race can be suppressed without being fixed and will return when something else changes.</p><p>This is why the effort goes into reproduction first. Once you can trigger it reliably, verifying the fix is trivial: it happened before the change and does not after. Without a reliable trigger you are relying on absence of evidence, which is exactly the situation that produces a fault returning six months later.</p>" },

// ---------------------------------------------------------- emb-power
{ t: "emb-power", d: 2, q: "You halve the CPU clock. Which effect do people most often forget?", o: ["The UART baud rate changes with it", "Timer periods double in wall time", "Every ISR takes twice as long to run", "Delay loops lengthen proportionally"], a: 2, why: "<p>The first three all happen, and baud rates and timer periods are the ones people remember because they are visibly clock-derived.</p><p>The compute budget is the one that gets missed, because nothing in the code refers to it. A handler that took 40 microseconds now takes 80, and if its deadline was 50, it is now missing it every time.</p><p>Nothing changed in the source, so the fault looks unrelated to the power-saving change that caused it. This is why dynamic frequency scaling needs a lock or vote mechanism: sections with real-time constraints pin the frequency while they run.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-power", d: 2, q: "Firmware works at 9600 but not 115200 on the same board. Most likely cause?", o: ["A broken or unusually long serial cable run", "The clock is not what the divider assumed", "The UART peripheral itself is faulty", "The parity setting differs at that rate"], a: 1, why: "<p>The divider is computed from an assumed clock. If the PLL failed to lock and the part fell back to its internal RC, the actual clock is different and every derived rate is wrong by that ratio.</p><p>It shows at high baud first because the divider is small, so rounding costs proportionally more, and UART only tolerates about two to three per cent across both ends.</p><p>Verify the clock rather than inferring it: output it on the MCO or clock-out pin and measure, or toggle a GPIO in a known loop and check the period. A PLL that fails to lock usually falls back silently, which is why everything appears to run.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-power", d: 2, q: "What must be configured before entering deep sleep?", o: ["The stack pointer", "The wake source", "The heap", "The watchdog"], a: 1, why: "<p>In deep sleep most of the chip is powered down and only specifically armed sources can wake it: an RTC alarm, a configured GPIO, occasionally a low-power comparator or watchdog.</p><p>Enter it with nothing armed and the part will not wake. Only a power cycle or a reset pin recovers it, which on a sealed battery product in the field means it is dead.</p><p>Two related traps: the debug connection usually drops in sleep unless the debug-in-sleep bit is set, which makes it look like a crash; and pin states may need configuring before entry, since a floating input can draw current that defeats the whole exercise.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-power", d: 2, q: "On a battery device, what usually dominates average current?", o: ["How efficient the code is while awake", "How long the part stays awake at all", "How often flash is read and written", "How much RAM is retained in sleep"], a: 1, why: "<p>Active current is typically milliamps while sleep current is microamps, often three orders of magnitude apart. So the average is dominated by the fraction of time spent awake.</p><p>Halving the execution time of the code while awake helps only in proportion to how much of the wake period is compute. Sleeping sooner, and waking less often, moves the number far more.</p><p>This is why DMA matters for battery devices: the core sleeps through the transfer instead of spinning on a peripheral. And it is why the first step in a power investigation is measuring the current profile over time rather than optimising code.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-power", d: 3, q: "What happens to your variables across deep sleep?", o: ["They are preserved exactly as they were", "Most RAM is lost; wake resembles a reset", "They are written to flash automatically", "They are zeroed but the addresses hold"], a: 1, why: "<p>Deep sleep powers down most of the RAM to reach microamp currents. Typically a small retention region stays powered, on the order of a few kilobytes.</p><p>So waking looks like a reset: startup runs, .data is copied, .bss is zeroed, and your state is gone unless you put it somewhere that survived.</p><p>Anything that must persist goes in the retention region, declared with a section attribute, or in non-volatile storage. And startup has to distinguish a cold boot from a wake, usually via the reset reason register, because the two need different initialisation.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-power", d: 2, q: "Why is the internal RC oscillator a poor choice above about 38400 baud?", o: ["It is too slow to clock the UART that fast", "One to two per cent error eats the budget", "It cannot drive the UART peripheral", "It draws more power at high baud"], a: 1, why: "<p>UART has no clock line, so the receiver recovers timing from the start bit and then free-runs, sampling the middle of each subsequent bit. Accumulated error walks the sampling point across the bit, and by the stop bit it can be sampling the wrong bit entirely.</p><p>The total budget across both ends is roughly two to three per cent. An RC oscillator alone can consume most of that, and it drifts further with temperature and supply voltage.</p><p>Add divider rounding, which grows as the divider shrinks, and high baud rates have nothing left. A crystal or a calibrated source is the fix; some parts allow trimming the RC against an external reference.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-power", d: 3, q: "What does a power management lock or vote do?", o: ["It reduces the current the core draws", "Pins the clock while a critical section runs", "Disables sleep for the whole application build", "Raises the priority of the calling task"], a: 1, why: "<p>It is a reference count. A driver takes a lock saying 'do not change the clock or enter sleep while I am doing this', and releases it afterwards. Power management only acts when nothing holds a lock.</p><p>Without it, a driver that computes a baud divider or a timing loop from the current frequency works perfectly until somebody enables dynamic scaling six months later.</p><p>The failure then looks completely unrelated to the change that caused it, and it appears intermittently, only when the scaling happens to occur mid-operation. Taking the lock is a few lines and it makes the dependency explicit rather than implicit.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-power", d: 1, q: "Which sleep mode retains RAM but stops most clocks?", o: ["Idle", "Sleep or stop", "Deep sleep, or standby", "Off"], a: 1, why: "<p>The ladder runs roughly: idle stops the core but leaves peripherals clocked, waking almost instantly; sleep or stop halts most clocks while keeping RAM and register state, waking in microseconds to milliseconds; deep sleep powers down most of the chip, losing RAM outside the retention region and waking like a reset.</p><p>Each step down saves an order of magnitude of current and costs wake latency and state.</p><p>Naming differs between vendors, which is why it is worth reading the actual power modes table rather than assuming your part's 'stop' matches another's. The trade being made, current against wake time against retained state, is the same everywhere.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-power", d: 2, q: "If the PLL fails to lock, what typically happens?", o: ["The part halts and waits for lock forever", "It runs on the fallback, so timing is wrong", "It resets and tries the sequence again", "The watchdog fires almost immediately"], a: 1, why: "<p>Most parts continue from the internal RC or the raw crystal rather than stopping. So the system boots, runs, and appears healthy.</p><p>But every clock-derived number is wrong by the ratio between the assumed and actual frequency. Baud rates are off, timer periods are off, delays are off, and the symptoms look like unrelated peripheral bugs.</p><p>Check the lock bit after configuring the PLL rather than assuming, and handle the failure explicitly. Measuring the clock on the MCO pin during bring-up is a cheap way to confirm it once and for all.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-power", d: 2, q: "Best first step when asked to halve a product's power consumption?", o: ["Lower the core clock, and then measure again", "Measure the current profile for duty cycle", "Optimise the main loop for more speed", "Reduce the radio transmit power"], a: 1, why: "<p>Measure before optimising. A current probe or a logging supply shows current against time, and the answer is usually somewhere nobody predicted: a peripheral left enabled, a pin floating and drawing current, a sensor never put into its low-power mode, a task polling instead of blocking.</p><p>The three other options are all guesses, and each has a real cost: lowering the clock breaks real-time deadlines, and reducing radio power costs range.</p><p>Once you have the profile, the biggest lever is almost always the duty cycle rather than the efficiency of any individual piece of work.</p>" },

// ---------------------------------------------------------- emb-build
{ t: "emb-build", d: 2, q: "Why does .data have two addresses in the linker script?", o: ["Redundancy in case flash is corrupt", "A load address in flash, run address in RAM", "One for the debug build, one for release", "For bank switching on larger parts"], a: 1, why: "<p>Initialised globals have to be writable, which means RAM, and their initial values have to survive power-off, which means flash. Those requirements need two locations.</p><p>The load address, LMA, is where the image is stored in flash. The run address, VMA, is where the variables live at execution. Startup copies from one to the other before main.</p><p>In a GNU linker script this appears as the AT&gt; keyword: .data section placed in RAM AT&gt; FLASH. The symbols marking the start and end are what the startup memcpy uses, which is why they must agree between the script and the startup file.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-build", d: 2, q: "Your firmware grows 40 kB after one library call. What tells you why?", o: ["The size of the resulting ELF file", "The map file, which attributes the space", "The disassembly of the new function", "The linker script's own section layout rules"], a: 1, why: "<p>The map file lists every symbol, its size, and which object file or archive member it came from, plus a cross-reference showing what pulled each one in.</p><p>The classic culprit is printf with floating point support, which drags in a substantial chunk of the C library. Sorting the map by size finds it in a minute.</p><p>The ELF size tells you the total but not the cause. Learning to read a map file is one of those skills that pays for itself repeatedly, because it also answers where your RAM went and why a symbol you thought was unused is present.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-build", d: 1, q: "What does the MEMORY block in a linker script declare?", o: ["Which sections the image contains", "Regions with origin, length, permissions", "The size reserved for the heap", "Where the initial stack pointer sits"], a: 1, why: "<p>MEMORY describes the hardware: FLASH at 0x08000000 with a length of 256K and rx permissions, RAM at 0x20000000 with 64K and rwx.</p><p>SECTIONS then maps output sections into those regions, and that is where a section acquires its load and run addresses.</p><p>The permissions are what produce the 'region RAM overflowed' error when .data and .bss exceed the declared length. Note that the heap and stack are usually not placed explicitly: they occupy whatever remains, which is why growing .bss silently shrinks the heap with no diagnostic.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-build", d: 2, q: "Why is developing at -O0 and shipping at -O2 risky?", o: ["-O2 is unstable on some compilers", "Missing volatile and UB only appear at -O2", "The debug symbols differ between the two builds", "It changes the memory map completely"], a: 1, why: "<p>At -O0 the compiler does not hoist reads, does not elide writes, and does not exploit undefined behaviour to delete branches. So both fault classes are invisible.</p><p>Turn on -O2 for the release and they appear, at the worst possible moment: after development, often in code nobody has touched for weeks, in a build that has had less testing than the one you have been running all along.</p><p>Develop at the level you ship, or at least run the test suite at it regularly. -Og is a reasonable everyday compromise, giving most of the optimisation with usable debugging.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-build", d: 2, q: "What is -Og for?", o: ["Optimising the final image for size", "Optimised but still debuggable", "Generating data for profiling", "Turning off warnings during a build"], a: 1, why: "<p>It enables optimisations that do not badly damage the debugging experience: variables mostly stay inspectable, the line order roughly matches the source, and stepping behaves sensibly.</p><p>At -O2 aggressive inlining and reordering mean the debugger shows a sequence of lines that did not happen in that order, and half your variables report as optimised out.</p><p>At -O0 the code is slow enough to change real-time behaviour, and it hides the volatile and UB faults you want to find early. -Og sits between the two and is the right default for day-to-day work.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-build", d: 2, q: "On a flash-constrained part, which level is often the right release setting?", o: ["-O0", "-O1", "-O2", "-Os"], a: 3, why: "<p>-Os is -O2 with the transformations that increase code size disabled, notably aggressive inlining and loop unrolling.</p><p>When the binary is near the partition limit, or when an OTA scheme needs two slots to fit, size is the binding constraint and a few per cent of speed is irrelevant.</p><p>It can occasionally be faster too, because a smaller working set behaves better in an instruction cache or prefetch buffer. -Oz on Clang goes further still. As always, measure both size and the timing that actually matters rather than assuming.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-build", d: 3, q: "Why pin the toolchain version alongside the source for a release?", o: ["The licence terms require the version", "So a release can be rebuilt years later", "It makes incremental builds faster", "CMake requires an exact version match"], a: 1, why: "<p>Source alone does not determine the binary. The compiler version, the flags, the C library version and the linker script all contribute, and a different compiler can produce meaningfully different code from identical source.</p><p>When a customer reports a fault in a two-year-old release, you need to rebuild that exact binary to reproduce and debug it. If the toolchain has moved on, you cannot.</p><p>The wider issue is that a build which only works on one engineer's laptop is a liability. Pinning the toolchain, and having CI build on a clean machine, is what turns the build into an asset the company owns.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-build", d: 2, q: "What does objcopy do in a typical firmware build?", o: ["It links the object files together", "Strips the ELF into a raw binary or hex", "It generates the linker's map file", "It runs the preprocessor over the sources"], a: 1, why: "<p>The linker produces an ELF, which contains the code and data plus symbols, debug information and section metadata. That is what the debugger needs.</p><p>The flash programmer wants only the bytes that go on the device, so objcopy extracts them into a .bin or Intel .hex.</p><p>Keeping both is the point: flash the .bin, archive the .elf. The ELF is what lets you decode a fault address from the field a year later, and it is useless if you did not keep the one that matches the shipped image.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-build", d: 2, q: "Which task most commonly requires editing a linker script?", o: ["Changing the optimisation level used", "Reserving a region, or placing code in RAM", "Adding another source file to build", "Changing which warnings are enabled"], a: 1, why: "<p>Placement is the linker script's job and nothing else can do it. Reserving a flash region for calibration data so the erase does not destroy it, placing a time-critical ISR in RAM so it runs during a flash write, carving out a retention region that survives deep sleep, or setting a bootloader offset.</p><p>All of these are decisions about where things live, expressed as section placement and often a section attribute in the source.</p><p>Everything else in the list is a compiler flag or a build system entry. Knowing which layer owns which decision is what stops you looking in the wrong file.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-build", d: 3, q: "Why does CI compiling every commit on a clean machine matter?", o: ["It is faster than building locally", "It proves the build needs no local state", "It reduces the size of the binary", "It replaces the need for a full test suite"], a: 1, why: "<p>A build accumulates undeclared dependencies: a tool installed years ago, an environment variable set in someone's profile, a stale object file, a header found because of a path that only exists on one machine.</p><p>None of those are visible until a machine that has never seen the project tries to build it, which is usually the worst possible moment: a new starter, a laptop replacement, or a release under time pressure.</p><p>CI on a clean checkout makes that failure happen on the commit that introduced it, when it is one change to look at rather than a year of accumulation.</p>" }

]);
